From: =?UTF-8?q?=C3=98yvind=20Harboe?= <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ram: the read side's drive, per eight words and per eight
 bits
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

With -decoder word, a port's address literals came from one inverter per
address bit, the complement, and from the address itself: each drove the
decoders of half the port's words, 16 of 32. An AND-OR read's word
select, one buffer, drove a read gate in every bit of the word, 32 of
32. In a flow the read path through them was the worst path left once
the write side was buffered, and repair_timing sized them up in CTS and
global route.

A port that decodes more than eight words now takes its literals from a
buffer and an inverter per group of eight words, in the decoder cell of
the group's first word; its column inverters are not made. A read port's
word select reaches the word's bits a group of eight at a time, a buffer
a group from the decoder. No literal or read select drives more than
eight pins. Not where -register_read_address keeps the address's
complement in its flop.

Carried in bazel-orfs, not upstreamed; retires with the //:bump onto an
OpenROAD that has it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Øyvind Harboe <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
